## Features

- `Style` names 68 recipes and the InvUI entry point names six of its own, each addressable by
  itself, so narrowing a run to one phase is a one-line change.
- The InvUI migration rewrites the build files first, then the types, then the method names. A
  rename keyed on a type has to run after that type has moved package, and the order in the recipe
  list is what guarantees it.
- **What it cannot decide, it marks.** `FindMethods` and `FindTypes` leave a `~~>` marker at every
  call site whose meaning changed under 2.x and at every type 2.x removed, instead of rewriting it
  into something that compiles and misbehaves.
- The dependency move covers the Gradle and Maven declarations plus three version-catalog aliases,
  `invui`, `invui-core` and `xenondevs-invui`, because a plugin project could have used any of them.
- Fourteen Java recipes, fourteen test classes. Each test compiles a fixture, runs the recipe over
  it and asserts the resulting source byte for byte.
- Every pull request builds and tests on JDK 21 and JDK 25.

## Installation

### Gradle

```kotlin
plugins {
    id("org.openrewrite.rewrite") version "7.39.0"
}

repositories {
    mavenCentral()
}

dependencies {
    rewrite("{{ repo.package }}:{{ release.version }}")
}
```

The plugin version above is your build's choice, not this project's. The current release is on the
[Gradle Plugin Portal](https://plugins.gradle.org/plugin/org.openrewrite.rewrite).

### Maven

Trying a recipe needs no POM edit. Maven resolves the plugin's own latest release:

```bash
mvn -U org.openrewrite.maven:rewrite-maven-plugin:run \
  -Drewrite.recipeArtifactCoordinates={{ repo.package }}:{{ release.version }} \
  -Drewrite.activeRecipes=de.timscho.rewrite.Style
```

The same two values go into a `<plugin>` block when the run has to be repeatable, as the
[rewrite-maven-plugin reference](https://docs.openrewrite.org/reference/rewrite-maven-plugin)
describes.

## Usage

### Preview, then apply

```bash
./gradlew rewriteDryRun   # writes build/reports/rewrite/rewrite.patch and nothing else
./gradlew rewriteRun      # applies the same changes to the source tree
```

Under Maven those are `rewrite:dryRun` and `rewrite:run`.

### Migrating InvUI

```kotlin
rewrite {
    activeRecipe("de.timscho.rewrite.minecraft.invui.v1-to-v2")
}
```

One run moves the InvUI dependency to 2.x, relocates the twelve types that changed package, applies
thirteen method renames, runs twelve Java visitors for the changes that need to read the syntax
tree, and drops the imports that go unused afterwards.

Then read the patch for `~~>`. Each marker is an API whose behaviour changed or that 2.x removed,
and each one is a decision the recipe declines to make for you.
[docs/RECIPES.md](docs/RECIPES.md) lists every one of them.

### Running a single recipe

Every recipe is addressable on its own, which is how you narrow a large diff:

```kotlin
rewrite {
    activeRecipe("de.timscho.rewrite.minecraft.invui.RemoveSimpleItemWrapper")
}
```

Two of the fourteen Java recipes are only reachable this way. `RemoveSimpleItemWrapper` is one.
The other is `MigrateAnvilWindowToOldApi`, which runs the other direction and rewrites
`AnvilWindow` from 2.x back to 1.x.

## Configuration

`activeRecipe` is the whole interface. No recipe here declares an option, so the only thing to
decide is which names to activate.

| Name | What activating it does |
| --- | --- |
| `de.timscho.rewrite.Style` | Runs 68 static-analysis recipes over the source set. |
| `de.timscho.rewrite.minecraft.invui.v1-to-v2` | Runs the whole InvUI migration and its follow-up markers. |
| `de.timscho.rewrite.minecraft.invui.v1-to-v2.Dependencies` and its five siblings | Runs one phase of that migration. |
| `de.timscho.rewrite.minecraft.invui.MigrateGuiToNewApi` and its thirteen siblings | Runs one Java recipe. |

Both YAML recipes ship inside the catalog jar under `META-INF/rewrite`, so one `rewrite` dependency
puts every name above on the classpath. [docs/RECIPES.md](docs/RECIPES.md) spells the siblings out.

